<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Nyerere</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Nyerere was a Tanzanian teacher and politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">Tanganyika African National Union</a> to independence in 1961. He became Prime Minister and then President of Tanganyika, and in 1964 united it with Zanzibar to form Tanzania, which he led until 1985. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-socialism">African socialism</a> through ujamaa villages, built national unity around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and supported liberation movements in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 1979 his army overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in Uganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere.jpg" alt="Julius Nyerere" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Mieremet / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Nyerere_van_Tanzania,_koppen,_Bestanddeelnr_928-2879_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julius Kambarage Nyerere was born on 13 April 1922 in Mwitongo, part of the village of Butiama in Tanganyika's Mara Region. He was one of 25 surviving children of Nyerere Burito, chief of the Zanaki people. The colonial administration encouraged the education of chiefs' sons, and at his father's prompting Nyerere began school in February 1934 at the Native Administration School in Mwisenge, Musoma.</p><p>In January 1943 he arrived at Makerere College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> on a bursary for teacher training. After teaching at the mission-run St Mary's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>, he went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> in April 1949 and took an Ordinary Master of Arts. He later said he had evolved the whole of his political philosophy there. He returned in October 1952, married Maria Gabriel in January 1953 and taught history at St Francis' College.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In April 1953 Nyerere became president of the Tanganyika African Association, which under him turned to the pursuit of independence, and in 1954 he helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">TANU</a>. Influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, he preached non-violence and said that TANU was fighting colonialism, not the whites. Tanganyika gained independence on 9 December 1961, and six weeks later he resigned as prime minister to restructure TANU. It became a republic on 9 December 1962, with Nyerere as president.</p><p>After the Zanzibar Revolution Tanganyika united with Zanzibar as Tanzania in 1964. In January 1967 Nyerere presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha-declaration">Arusha Declaration</a>, committing the government to democratic socialism and self-reliance. Banks and major industries were nationalised and education and health care expanded. In the early 1970s villagisation moved about 13 million people into around 7,000 villages, and forced moves disrupted rural production; none of the villages had met productivity targets by 1978, and many depended on famine relief. His government also trained and aided movements fighting white-minority rule in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and Tanzania's 1978–1979 war with Uganda overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nyerere stepped down as president in 1985, succeeded by Ali Hassan Mwinyi, who reversed many of his policies. He remained chair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chama-cha-mapinduzi">Chama Cha Mapinduzi</a> until 1990. He died on 14 October 1999. Within Tanzania he is called the Father of the Nation.</p><p>Admirers praise him as an anti-colonialist and for keeping Tanzania stable and unified; critics point to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and detention without trial, and blame him for economic mismanagement. In the late 1990s he said he had made mistakes, particularly in pursuing nationalisation too early, but stood by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha-declaration">Arusha Declaration</a>. In 2005 the Diocese of Musoma opened the cause for his canonisation, with the title Servant of God.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julius Nyerere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julius Nyerere</b> translated Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar and The Merchant of Venice into Swahili, part of his effort to make Swahili a language of national culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Julius Nyerere</b>, the first president of Tanzania, translated Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar and The Merchant of Venice into Swahili, to show that the language could carry any literature in the world.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, the Anglican priest Trevor Huddleston, who had worked in Sophiatown until 1956, became president of the British Anti-Apartheid Movement, and <b>Julius Nyerere</b> of Tanzania and Kenneth Kaunda of Zambia gave the exiled movements bases.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Julius Nyerere</b> favoured regional federations as a first step, and most leaders preferred cooperation among sovereign states.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…newly independent states in Africa and Asia adopted single-party rule, arguing that national unity and development came before competition: Ghana under Kwame Nkrumah from 1964, Tanzania under <b>Julius Nyerere</b> from 1965, Zambia from 1972 and Kenya from 1982, as well as Egypt, Algeria, Tunisia, Ivory Coast and Burma.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…H. Friedland and Carl G. Rosberg Jr., the editors of the book African Socialism, its main architects were <b>Julius Nyerere</b> in Tanzania, Léopold Senghor in Senegal, Joseph Saidu Momoh and Siaka Stevens in Sierra Leone, Kwame Nkrumah and Hilla Limann in Ghana, François Tombalbaye in Chad, Modibo Keïta…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/african-socialism">African socialism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Butiama, in what is now <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-1.77,33.97&amp;year=1972" aria-label="Butiama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5493 2230 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julius Nyerere"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5943 2500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5943 2500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-1.77,33.97&amp;year=1972">What was here in 1972? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
